# No invented customer-facing product surfaces

Standing rule for this entire program (not scoped to one prompt).

## Never invent

Do **not** invent, seed, or fill in as plausible-looking example data any:

- customer-visible **price**
- **claim** (compliance, capability, certification, “live” status)
- **badge** (TRAINED, certified, included, etc.)
- **capability toggle** (Enable / Disable that looks billable or entitlement-real)
- other **product surface** that a real customer could mistake for authorized product

…unless the user **explicitly, separately requested** it and **confirmed it as real** in that conversation.

## If placeholder is genuinely required

Demo / scaffold / visual mockup data must be **structurally impossible** to ship to a real customer:

1. Kept entirely out of any customer-facing route, **or**
2. Visibly, unmistakably labeled **placeholder** in the UI itself (not only a code comment)

Never give placeholders a **real-looking price** or a **working Enable** action.

## Before commit — declare in the delivery report

For any commit that adds a new customer-facing price, feature claim, or toggle, state explicitly in the **same delivery message**:

- **(a)** Explicitly requested and authorized in this specific conversation, **or**
- **(b)** Generated to fill out a broader scaffold

Anything under **(b)** must be flagged as exactly that, honestly, when delivered — not discovered months later.

## Proven failure class (do not repeat)

Five instances from the 2026-04 monetization / integration period: Meson scaffolding SKUs, environment mock fallback, SOC 2 marketing overclaims, fake TRAINED / silent confidence, ROI placeholders presented as live. Same class: AI-assisted build invents plausible product surface without product authorization.
